cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "WiFi-Radar-for-SLAM: ambient WiFi for vehicular multipath SLAM (Sionna RT simulation + ESP32 hardware)"
abstract: >-
  A ray-traced (Sionna RT) study, with ESP32-S3 hardware, of whether ambient
  sub-7 GHz WiFi received on a moving vehicle can aid localization and mapping.
  Using wheel-grade odometry as the baseline, a virtual-anchor FastSLAM back-end
  and a LiDAR comparison, exact multipath parameters reduce odometry drift by
  66-79 %, while a commodity four-element joint-MUSIC front-end returns too few
  facade reflections and too many persistent false detections, making WiFi-aided
  SLAM worse than odometry alone. Access-point positions must be known to
  sub-metre accuracy. The work also documents a delay-convention pitfall in
  ray-traced CSI (first-arrival-referenced delays read as absolute path lengths)
  that invalidated earlier versions of this study, whose localization-parity and
  phantom-ceiling claims are withdrawn.
